Now An application may require the user-supplied filename to end with an expected file extension, such as .png/.jpg.
Even if a website forces your file name to end with .png/.jpg, you can put a hidden "stop sign" (%00) right before .png/.jpg so the server ignores .png/.jpg and opens the secret file instead.
%00 is the URL-encoded null byte - Character '0'

